sg-session (0.1.0-8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Network drives (D1): sg-netmountd mounts \\server\share for a user with
    their own Kerberos ticket (kernel CIFS, multiuser, so the file server
    checks each user) under /run/stained-glass-net/unc, and keeps each
    user's drive letters in /run/stained-glass-net/drives/<uid>. Socket
    activated (sg-netmountd.socket); wine-sg 0056/0057 use it.
  * sg-domain-logon (pam_exec, with sg-domain-groups): a domain user's home
    drive (homeDirectory/homeDrive) is mapped at sign-in and their logon
    script (scriptPath, from NETLOGON) recorded; the session runs it hidden
    as the desktop starts. Drive letters go with the last session
    (user-runtime-dir@.service drop-in).
  * sg-gpo-user: a domain user's Group Policy at sign-in -- GPP drive maps,
    GPO logon scripts, user Registry.pol into HKCU -- from the GPOs Samba's
    get_gpo_list selects, read from SYSVOL with the user's own ticket.
  * The domain logon variables (USERDOMAIN, LOGONSERVER, HOMEDRIVE/SHARE/PATH)
    go into Volatile Environment before the session starts.
  * The Start menu and logon scripts start once the shell owns the desktop:
    started first, they made Wine create the desktop itself, and the shell's
    explorer exited at once, which the supervisor took for a sign-out.
  * Remote Desktop takes over a session signed in at the console (E1b).

 -- Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:17:14 -0700

sg-session (0.1.0-7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The machine's wineserver owns the prefix at boot: sg-prefix-init stops the
    transient server its own Wine commands started (its wait is bounded), and
    sg-wineserver stops any server still holding the prefix before starting.
    Once a prefix has auto-start services -- Microsoft Edge installs three --
    they kept the transient server alive, the machine's server found the
    prefix taken and never started, and the login screen came up on the wrong
    server.

